# SuperHeng frontend — environment template
#
#   cp .env.example .env      # macOS / Linux
#   copy .env.example .env    # Windows
#
# `.env` is git-ignored; this template is safe to commit.

# Base URL the app calls for predictions.
#
# Expo inlines EXPO_PUBLIC_* variables into the bundle at BUILD time, not at
# runtime — the value below is baked into `dist/` by `npm run build`. Editing it
# after a build changes nothing until you rebuild and redeploy, and it is
# readable by anyone who opens the site, so never put a secret here.
#
# Omit the variable entirely for local development and src/lib/api.ts falls back
# to http://127.0.0.1:8000 (correct for the web browser and the iOS simulator;
# use http://10.0.2.2:8000 for the Android emulator, or your machine's LAN IP for
# a physical phone). Point it at the deployed API for the hosted build:
#
#   EXPO_PUBLIC_API_URL=https://superheng-api.onrender.com
#
# No trailing slash — the request path is appended directly.
EXPO_PUBLIC_API_URL=https://superheng-api.onrender.com

# --- Accounts (premium subscriptions) ---------------------------------------
# Required for the Premium panel to be usable at all. Without these two the app
# still runs — the dashboard, the ledger and the backtest stay free and the
# Premium card says accounts are not configured — but nobody can sign in.
#
#   cp .env.example .env      # then fill both in
#
# Supabase dashboard > Project Settings > API Keys:
#
#   EXPO_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL      https://<project-ref>.supabase.co  (no trailing slash)
#   EXPO_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY the **publishable / anon** key
#
# The anon key is meant to be public: Expo inlines it into the bundle like
# EXPO_PUBLIC_API_URL, and row-level security is what protects the data behind it
# (`backend/schema_profiles.sql`). NEVER put the service_role key here — it
# bypasses RLS, and the whole point of the premium gate's design is that the API
# reads an entitlement through RLS with the *user's own* token and therefore
# cannot be tricked into reading someone else's row.
#
# Both are read at BUILD time. Editing them after a build changes nothing until
# you rebuild, which is why a hosted deployment sets them as Pages build
# variables rather than runtime ones. See DEPLOY.md, step 6.
EXPO_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=https://your-project-ref.supabase.co
EXPO_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=your-supabase-publishable-anon-key

# --- Payments (Stripe) -------------------------------------------------------
# **Nothing to add here**, and that is the answer to the obvious question.
#
# The integration brief named `NEXT_PUBLIC_STRIPE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY` among the
# variables to configure. There is deliberately no such variable in this
# project, for two reasons:
#
#   1. This is an **Expo** app, not Next.js, so the `NEXT_PUBLIC_` prefix would
#      do nothing at all. Expo inlines `EXPO_PUBLIC_*` at build time; any other
#      prefix is read as undefined at runtime.
#   2. More to the point, **a publishable key is not needed** for this
#      integration. The app asks the API for a Stripe Checkout URL and redirects
#      the browser to Stripe's own hosted page. No Stripe.js, no
#      `@stripe/stripe-react-native`, no Payment Element — nothing Stripe-related
#      runs inside the app, so there is no key for the app to hold.
#
# A publishable key would become necessary only to render the payment form
# *in-process* (Payment Element on web, PaymentSheet on native). That is a
# different integration: it needs a native module, which means the app can no
# longer run in Expo Go and every tester needs a development build. Hosted
# Checkout keeps card details off this project's origin as well, which is the
# better trade for a ฿99 pass.
#
# So the Stripe credentials live in backend/.env only, because the API is the
# only thing that talks to Stripe: `STRIPE_SECRET_KEY` and
# `STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET`. See backend/.env.example.
